--!strict
--[[
	enemy/Boar/Walk: a 0.3 s trot on the boar's rigid legs (D-482), paced to rigs.luau Boar walkSpeed
	14 (RigDriver plays it at speed / walkSpeed). Diagonal pairs (front-left with back-right) plant
	together: each planted leg rolls over its sole, heel edge then toe edge, under a hip that moves
	forward at the walk's speed, so the edge on the field holds its place (33.2 deg forward to 33.2
	back, 2.10 studs a stance). The body rides the stiff legs' arc, 0.074 studs below to 0.044 above
	the stance height. The other pair swings through, its hips drawn 0.20 studs up into the body at
	mid-swing (a leg with no knee lifts its sole that way), so the swinging sole clears the field by
	0.042 or more. The head nods against the bob, the tail swishes once a stride. Footsteps on the
	plants.
	Generated: one key a 60 fps frame (the stance angle solved from the distance walked), then each
	frame split into four SineInOut keys on the straight line between its two keys (D-576), so a
	planted sole moves near evenly between the 60 fps keys and a 144 Hz row no longer catches it
	stopping and going inside one ease (the boar wheeling at 90 deg with the gait on the replicated
	velocity: 0.073 a 144 Hz row and 19 rows past 0.042 on one key a frame, 0.058 and 2 rows on
	four). The first frame's first quarter keeps the single ease's own pace (its key stands where
	that ease stood at 1/240 s), so the cut-in over the fading idle ClipAudit's Lab plays reads as
	before (Drift 0.46, PENDING). Rig: Boar. Hip X+ swings a leg forward; a hip's pos Y draws the
	leg up into the body.
]]

local Types = require(script.Parent.Parent.Parent.Parent.types)

local function rot(x: number, y: number, z: number): Types.JointKey
	return { rot = { x, y, z } }
end

local function posRot(px: number, py: number, pz: number, rx: number, ry: number, rz: number): Types.JointKey
	return { pos = { px, py, pz }, rot = { rx, ry, rz } }
end

